Subject: ASoC: codecs: msm8916-wcd-analog: ground the TX negative inputs on PM8916

CDC_A_MICB_1_INT_RBIAS powers up as 0x49: the internal pull-ups of
TX1N, TX2N and TX3N are enabled, tying each microphone channel's negative
input to MIC BIAS. The PM8950 and PM8953 register sequences write 0x00 to
it, the PM8916 sequence never touches it, so on PM8916 the bias rail sits
on the inverting input of every analog mic and any noise on it is
captured differentially.

On the Samsung Galaxy Tab A 9.7 (SM-T550) the primary mic (AMIC1, MIC BIAS
External1) recorded a ~16 kHz tone at -11 dBFS at full ADC gain, its
frequency wandering by ~150 Hz and its level following the ADC1 gain;
clearing the three pull-up bits at runtime dropped it by 58 dB to the
noise floor (-75 dB in the 14-18 kHz band) while a 1 kHz tone played on
the speaker was still captured at the same level. The downstream
msm8x16-wcd driver for this device writes 0x00 to the register too.

Write 0x00 like the newer PMIC sequences. The internal bias for the
headset mic (bit 4) is enabled later by the MBHC setup and the
"MIC BIAS Internal2" DAPM supply when needed.
---
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/msm8916-wcd-analog.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/msm8916-wcd-analog.c
@@ -780,6 +780,7 @@
 	{CDC_A_SPKR_DAC_CTL, 0x03},
 	{CDC_A_SPKR_OCP_CTL, 0xE1},
 	{CDC_A_MASTER_BIAS_CTL, 0x30},
+	{CDC_A_MICB_1_INT_RBIAS, 0x00},
 };
 
 static const struct wcd_reg_seq pm8916_data = {
